## 1. Ranked candidates

### 1. The chat-cache park of the previous 54K conversation inside R3's prompt time, and its two-level cost
- **Code (ours, not upstream):**
  - Commit `4036f229f` (r23; r22 `83ee4a276`), "serve: --chat-cache N keeps other conversations in RAM", plus
    `a7a45b11d`, the owner tag.
  - `park()` is at generate.cpp:15314-15381:
    - its timer starts at :15317;
    - `cudaDeviceSynchronize()` at :15318;
    - per host K/V array, `new uint8_t[bytes]` at :15328, then `par_copy` at :15329. `par_copy` (:15295-15305) is a
      single `memcpy` for any array of 64 MiB or less;
    - per QSA layer, `cudaMemcpy` of the indexer rows at :15339-15341;
    - the log line at :15351.
  - The stash and park block is the same text in both trees (r22 :13440-13720 vs r23 :15148-15428). The only
    difference is one pipelined-batch line, `pc.on()`, outside `park`.
- **Mechanism:**
  - R3 is a new conversation (nonce, 0 reused tokens), so the request parks R2's live conversation first: 54,067
    cells, 4 checkpoints, 1238 MiB. This happens after r0, so its time is part of R3's prompt time.
  - The bytes are the same on both builds (1238 MiB on both). The time is not:
    - B: 105 / 106 / 104 ms;
    - P: 125 / 126 / 126 ms;
    - passes interleaved B P P B B P, so drift does not explain the split.
  - R3's prefill itself is identical: B 1289 / 1288 / 1287 ms vs P 1288 / 1287 / 1291 ms.
- **The park cost has two levels on every build.** Across all 119 stored passes of this park (every gap since r22 gap 9):
  - fast: 102-113 ms (44 passes);
  - slow: 123-137 ms (75 passes);
  - nothing in 114-122.

  | build | passes at the fast level |
  |---|---|
  | x28h itself | 9 of 15 (gap16 X 130/103/131, gap17 B 137/106/135, gap18 B 134/107/107, ud31 P 111/105/130, today B 105/106/104) |
  | x28h switch variants (gap 18) | 11 of 17 |
  | v0.2.10 port family (gap 17: P / F / L / R) | 1 of 10 |
  | v0.2.11 port (today) | 0 of 3 |

  - The 24K and 26.7K parks (578 and 614 MiB) do not show the two levels: 47-56 ms in every stored pass.
  - The fast-level share differs between x28h (9 of 15) and the two ports (1 of 13): Fisher one-sided p = 0.005. That
    is suggestive, but sessions are confounded. Gap 17 had B at 1 of 3; today had B at 3 of 3.
- **Why a ~5K prompt and not 16K / 64K / 33K:**
  - The extra cost is about 20-27 ms and is fixed by the previous conversation's size, not by R3's. On R3's 1.29 s
    prefill that is 1.4-1.9 %.
  - Basalt's 16K / 64K fixtures run on cold engines, so no park is timed.
  - R4 parks only R3's 5K conversation (10 ms, one level).
  - R2 parks a 24K conversation (one level).
- **Size:**
  - It accounts for 20.7 of R3's 21.0 ms, i.e. -1.43 of the -1.45 points.
  - Against x28h's own 15-pass mean park (116.7 ms), P's R3 would be -0.6 %. Against x28h's slow-level passes, P is
    faster: 3,485 vs 3,468 tok/s.
- **Earlier gate (0.2.8 -> 0.2.10 vs 0.2.10 -> 0.2.11):**
  - Gap 17 had the same mechanism: the v0.2.10 port's R3 was -0.77 % at the client. It passed only because B itself
    drew 137 / 106 / 135, so the noise was 2.09 %.
  - With the park taken out, gap 17's R3 is -0.22 % (noise 0.83 %).
  - So there is no 0.2.8 -> 0.2.10 or 0.2.10 -> 0.2.11 code step to split.
- **Switch or pin:** none upstream; nothing to pin.
  - Test switch: `--chat-cache 0`, which takes the park out of R3's request.
  - Measurement fix: time the screen's prompt as engine prompt ms minus the `parked ... in N ms` line, or report the
    park on its own line. Our park is inside 5 of the 9 workloads' TTFT: A-edit 54 ms, B-write 6, R1 3, R2 48, R3
    105-126, R4 10.
  - Code options for later, not proposed now:
    - park after the previous request's DONE instead of inside the next request's prompt time;
    - keep reusable host buffers for parks.

### 2. A ~15 ms one-off outside the engine's prompt timer, moved from R1 (B) to R4 (P)
- **Data, client TTFT minus engine prompt ms, per pass:**
  - R4: B 45 / 45 / 44; P 60 / 61 / 63.
  - R1: B 60 / 58 / 58; P 46 / 43 / 46.
  - Every other workload agrees within 1 ms.
  - The R1 + R4 sum barely changes: B 103.4 ms, P 106.3 ms.
- **History** (same metric, stored gaps): the pause sits on R1, on R4 or on neither, depending on the build:

  | build | where the pause lands |
  |---|---|
  | x28h family | R1 at about 57-60 ms in most passes (all of gap 17, gap 18, ud31 and today; gap 16's X 1 of 3, Xc 2 of 3); R4 always about 45 |
  | v0.2.10 port (gap 17) | R1 about 44 in all 10 passes; R4 at 61 in 1 of 10 |
  | P021m arm M (gaps 15, 16) | R4 at 56-59 in 5 of 5 |
  | r27h | neither (gap 14); R1 at 58 in noboth (2 of 2) |

  So it is not a property of v0.2.11's prompt path, nor of R4's tools.
- **Mechanism (inferred, not verified):** a pause of one fixed size that lands on whichever request is being prepared
  when it fires. Its position depends on the server or engine code, not on the request.
  - The most likely source is an allocation-driven pause in the Python server's request preparation: CPython 3.12's
    cyclic GC during render and tokenize (`render_prompt` renders a 33K-token medium-effort request 3 times,
    server.py:5804-5821).
  - v0.2.11's server.py differs from v0.2.8's by 1,493 lines (90 commits, scratch `server.diff`), which shifts the
    allocation sequence.
  - The render and encode functions themselves are byte-identical (r23 server.py:5804-5852 vs r22 :4922-4970).
- **Size:** R4 -16.7 ms, -0.21 of its -0.29 points. R1 gains +13.7 ms (+0.25 %).
- **Switch or pin:** none. There is no upstream commit to point at.

### 3. Engine-side residual on R1 / R4: +5 ms (-0.07 % / -0.10 %), within noise
- **Data:** engine minus park is +5.3 ms on R4 and +5.7 on R1 today; gap 17 had +14.3 / +5.3. W3 (+0.7), R2 (+2.0)
  and R3 (+0.7) show nothing, so it does not scale with chunk count or prompt length. Today it passes the rule.
- **The one unpinned new default reachable on the prompt path:** the dispatch counters, `af6ba4c60` (v0.2.10).
  - They are on unless `STRATA_DISPATCH_STATS=0` (include/strata/core/dispatch_stats.hpp:115-117).
  - Each eager call takes one mutex and one hash lookup: the HC read at fused_gr.cu:1625 / :3173 and grouped experts
    at iq_kernels.cu:6276 / :6401. MMVQ at native_mmvq.cu:3569 / :3969 is decode.
  - Estimated well under 1 ms per prompt (tens of ns × 10²-10³ calls). Not expected to explain even this residual.
- **Switch or pin:** `STRATA_DISPATCH_STATS=0` gives identity if wanted. It is not a fix for R3 or R4.

